{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01005 - MONDO_16249",
  "verbalization": "Hydroxyurea is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hereditary Site-specific Ovarian Cancer Syndrome through a mechanism involving cisplatin, which is a known therapeutic agent for several ovarian cancers. The evidence suggests that Hydroxyurea may be linked to the development of ovarian cancers through a shared pathway involving cisplatin, as it is indicated for multiple ovarian malignancies, including Ovarian Endometrioid Adenocarcinoma, Malignant Sex Cord Stromal Tumor of Ovary, and Maligant Granulosa Cell Tumor of Ovary. This indicates that Hydroxyurea may influence the progression or risk of ovarian cancer by contributing to the development of these malignancies, which in turn are associated with cisplatin treatment. The presence of a consistent pathway linking Hydroxyurea to cisplatin, and subsequently to Hereditary Site-specific Ovarian Cancer Syndrome, supports the hypothesis that Hydroxyurea may play a role in the etiology of this syndrome. The mechanism is supported by the observed clinical indications and the shared therapeutic use of cisplatin across these malignancies, suggesting a potential biological link that warrants further investigation.",
